WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.001
Idea. When cakes sell for a higher price, a bakery bakes more of them. Economists ask: how strongly does supply react to price?

00:00:09.560 --> 00:00:25.185
Price elasticity of supply, or P E S, is the percentage change in quantity supplied divided by the percentage change in price. Price up ten per cent, quantity supplied up fifteen per cent: P E S is one point five.

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:37.043
Along a supply curve, price and quantity supplied move the same way. When demand rises, price rises and so does quantity supplied. So P E S is positive.

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:44.729
Read the size. Below one, supply is inelastic. Exactly one, unitary. Above one, elastic.

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:54.339
Method. Step one. Write both changes with signs. Say price falls eight per cent and quantity supplied falls four per cent.

00:00:54.920 --> 00:01:06.705
Steps two and three. Quantity supplied on top, price underneath. Minus four divided by minus eight is plus nought point five. A fall divided by a fall is positive.

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:21.059
If P E S is given, multiply it by the price change to find the change in quantity supplied. P E S of two and a three per cent price rise give a six per cent rise in quantity supplied.

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:39.950
Worked example. Here is a real question from June 2022. Greg makes pottery. The price rose by five point four per cent and quantity supplied rose by four point seven per cent. Calculate P E S to two decimal places. Two marks.

00:01:40.520 --> 00:01:47.538
Both are rises, so both are plus. Price plus five point four, quantity supplied plus four point seven.

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:58.701
Quantity on top. Four point seven divided by five point four is nought point eight seven zero three. To two decimal places, nought point eight seven.

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:13.145
Nought point eight seven is below one, so Greg's supply is price inelastic: output rose by a smaller percentage than price. This explanation is by olevels dot com, not an official mark scheme.

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:22.493
Common mistake. A common mistake is putting price on top. Five point four over four point seven gives the wrong answer.

00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:29.202
Another is giving a negative P E S when both changes are falls, or adding a per cent sign.

00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:49.935
Your turn. Now you try. A toy factory sees the price of its scooters rise by six per cent. It raises quantity supplied by nine point five per cent. Calculate P E S to two decimal places, and say if supply is elastic or inelastic. Pause here and work it out.

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:59.939
Answer. Nine point five divided by six is one point five eight three three. To two decimal places, one point five eight.

00:03:00.520 --> 00:03:07.601
One point five eight is above one, so supply is elastic. Quantity supplied rose by more than the price.

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:18.206
Remember: quantity supplied over price, divide, round as asked, and give a positive number with no per cent sign. Now try the practice questions.
